\DOC LOCAL_MOD_P521_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MOD_P521_TAC : int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-state goal across a called
modular-reduction subroutine, moving the goal and its state-dependent
assumptions from the current state variable to the next-numbered one.

\DESCRIBE
The single {int} argument is the index of the new state variable in which the
goal and assumptions are expressed after the step; it is the successor of the
index of the state the goal mentions beforehand. Applied to a goal whose
conclusion is an {eventually} assertion about a postcondition predicate,
together with a full set of state assumptions, the tactic yields exactly one
subgoal. The conclusion keeps its shape, with the state argument replaced by
the freshly indexed state. Every assumption mentioning the old state is
discharged and replaced by the corresponding assumption about the new state:
the program-image assumption, the preserved memory and register contents, a
program counter and stack pointer restored from the saved return address, and a
{MAYCHANGE} frame relating the initial state to the new state, extended with
the components written by the step. One further assumption is added, stating
that the destination buffer in memory now holds the reduced value corresponding
to the subroutine's input operand. Assumptions mentioning no machine state —
the size/nonoverlap side conditions and the arithmetic facts about the inputs —
are left untouched and keep their positions.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, on goals of
this one {eventually} form carrying a full set of state assumptions.

\ENDDOC
